 "literal": "Pollen-seeking livelihood beautiful-winged bee / bias without-speaking what-was-seen please-speak / closely-associated intimate friendship's peacock-like-grace / close-set teeth young-woman's than-the-hair / fragrant-things also are-there you known flowers?",
 "translation": "O beautiful-winged bee whose livelihood is seeking pollen! Do not speak out of bias or desire to please me, but tell me only what you have actually seen: among all the flowers known to you, are there any that are as fragrant as the hair of this young woman with close-set teeth and the graceful nature of a peacock, with whom I share a deep and intimate love?",
 "modern": "மகரந்தத்தைத் தேடி வாழும் அழகான சிறகுகளைக் கொண்ட வண்டே! என் மீதுள்ள விருப்பத்தால் பொய் கூறாமல், நீ கண்ட உண்மையை மட்டும் சொல்வாயாக. நீண்ட நாள் பழகிய நெருக்கமான அன்பையும், மயிலின் மென்மையையும், நெருக்கமான பற்களையும் உடைய என் தலைவியின் கூந்தலை விட நறுமணமுள்ள மலர்கள் நீ அறிந்த மலர்களுள் ஏதேனும் உண்டோ?",
 "explanation": "This famous poem belongs to the Kurinci (mountainous) landscape, representing the hero's utterance. Having met his beloved in the hills, he is overwhelmed by her beauty and the natural fragrance of her hair. To praise her and reassure himself of her divine perfection, he addresses a bee that flies from flower to flower. He asks the bee to speak honestly, without any bias or desire to flatter him, and tell him if any flower it knows has a fragrance sweeter than his beloved's hair. The imagery of the bee, the peacock-like grace of the woman, and the lush mountain flowers perfectly evoke the intimate, passionate atmosphere of Kurinci. Key words include 'தும்பி' (bee), 'கூந்தல்' (hair), and 'நறியவும்' (fragrant things).",
